Here's a C++ solution to the problem. The approach is straightforward:

1. **Input Handling**: First, the program reads the number of test cases `t`. For each test case, it reads a string that represents the current order of the cards.

2. **Check for "abc"**: 
    - If the string is already "abc", it outputs "YES".
    - If not, the program tries all possible single swaps of two cards to see if it can transform the string into "abc". Since there are only three cards, there are three possible swaps:
        - Swap the first and second card.
        - Swap the first and third card.
        - Swap the second and third card.

3. **Output**:
    - If any single swap results in "abc", the program outputs "YES".
    - If none of the swaps result in "abc", it outputs "NO".

This method ensures that the solution is efficient and handles all possible scenarios within the given constraints.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main(){
    int t;
    cin >> t;
    while(t--){
        string s;
        cin >> s;
        if(s == "abc"){
            cout << "YES\n";
            continue;
        }
        bool found = false;
        // Try all possible swaps
        for(int i=0;i<3 && !found;i++){
            for(int j=i+1;j<3 && !found;j++){
                string temp = s;
                swap(temp[i], temp[j]);
                if(temp == "abc"){
                    found = true;
                }
            }
        }
        if(found){
            cout << "YES\n";
        }
        else{
            cout << "NO\n";
        }
    }
}
```

Adding a Toolbar to a Fragment in Android involves several steps, including defining the Toolbar in your fragment's layout, initializing it in your fragment's code, and configuring it to work with your Activity, especially if you want it to behave like the Activity's ActionBar. Below is a comprehensive guide to help you achieve this.

## 1. Ensure You're Using the Correct Dependencies

First, make sure your project is set up to use the AndroidX libraries and the Material Components, as these provide enhanced support for modern UI components like `Toolbar`.

In your `build.gradle` (app module) file:

```groovy
dependencies {
    implementation 'androidx.appcompat:appcompat:1.6.1'
    implementation 'com.google.android.material:material:1.9.0'
    // Other dependencies...
}
```

## 2. Create the Fragment Layout with a Toolbar

Define a layout for your Fragment that includes the `Toolbar`. Here's an example using XML:

```xml
<!-- res/layout/fragment_example.xml -->
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    android:orientation="vertical"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent">

    <!-- Toolbar -->
    <androidx.appcompat.widget.Toolbar
        android:id="@+id/toolbar_fragment"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="?attr/actionBarSize"
        android:background="?attr/colorPrimary"
        app:title="Fragment Toolbar"
        app:titleTextColor="@android:color/white"
        app:popupTheme="@style/ThemeOverlay.AppCompat.Light" />

    <!-- Other fragment UI elements -->
    <FrameLayout
        android:id="@+id/fragment_container"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="0dp"
        android:layout_weight="1">
        <!-- Content goes here -->
    </FrameLayout>

</LinearLayout>
```

## 3. Set Up the Fragment Class

In your Fragment class, initialize the Toolbar and set it up to act as the ActionBar if desired.

```java
// ExampleFragment.java
import android.os.Bundle;
import android.view.LayoutInflater;
import android.view.Menu;
import android.view.MenuInflater;
import android.view.MenuItem;
import android.view.View;
import android.view.ViewGroup;
import androidx.annotation.NonNull;
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity;
import androidx.appcompat.widget.Toolbar;
import androidx.fragment.app.Fragment;

public class ExampleFragment extends Fragment {

    private Toolbar toolbar;

    @Override
    public void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        // Indicate that this fragment wants to add items to the Options Menu
        setHasOptionsMenu(true);
    }

    @Override
    public View onCreateView(@NonNull LayoutInflater inflater, ViewGroup container,
                             Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        // Inflate the fragment layout
        View view = inflater.inflate(R.layout.fragment_example, container, false);

        // Initialize the Toolbar
        toolbar = view.findViewById(R.id.toolbar_fragment);

        // If you want the Toolbar to act as the ActionBar for this fragment
        AppCompatActivity activity = (AppCompatActivity) getActivity();
        if (activity != null) {
            activity.setSupportActionBar(toolbar);
            // Optional: Set the title or other properties
            activity.getSupportActionBar().setTitle("Fragment Toolbar Title");
            activity.getSupportActionBar().setDisplayHomeAsUpEnabled(true);
        }

        return view;
    }

    @Override
    public void onCreateOptionsMenu(@NonNull Menu menu, @NonNull MenuInflater inflater) {
        // Inflate the menu; this adds items to the toolbar if it is present
        inflater.inflate(R.menu.fragment_menu, menu);
        super.onCreateOptionsMenu(menu, inflater);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean onOptionsItemSelected(@NonNull MenuItem item) {
        // Handle toolbar item clicks
        switch (item.getItemId()) {
            case android.R.id.home:
                // Handle the back button click here
                if (getActivity() != null) {
                    getActivity().onBackPressed();
                }
                return true;
            case R.id.action_settings:
                // Handle settings action
                return true;
            // Add more cases for other menu items
            default:
                return super.onOptionsItemSelected(item);
        }
    }
}
```

### Explanation of the Fragment Class

1. **`setHasOptionsMenu(true);`**: This tells the FragmentManager that the fragment would like to participate in populating the options menu by receiving a call to `onCreateOptionsMenu()`.

2. **Initializing the Toolbar**: The `Toolbar` is retrieved from the fragment's view and set as the support ActionBar of the hosting Activity using `setSupportActionBar(toolbar);`. This allows the Toolbar within the Fragment to behave like the Activity's ActionBar.

3. **Handling Menu Items**: Override `onCreateOptionsMenu()` and `onOptionsItemSelected()` to inflate and handle menu items specific to the Fragment.

## 4. Define the Fragment's Menu

Create a menu resource file for the Fragment's Toolbar.

```xml
<!-- res/menu/fragment_menu.xml -->
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<menu xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!-- Example menu items -->
    <item
        android:id="@+id/action_settings"
        android:title="Settings"
        android:icon="@android:drawable/ic_menu_preferences"
        android:showAsAction="ifRoom" />
    <item
        android:id="@+id/action_search"
        android:title="Search"
        android:icon="@android:drawable/ic_menu_search"
        android:showAsAction="ifRoom" />
</menu>
```

## 5. Host the Fragment in an Activity

Ensure that the Activity hosting the Fragment uses a `Toolbar` or supports one.

```xml
<!-- res/layout/activity_main.xml -->
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<FrameLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:id="@+id/main_container"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent">
    <!-- The fragment will be loaded here -->
</FrameLayout>
```

```java
// MainActivity.java
import android.os.Bundle;
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity;
import androidx.fragment.app.FragmentTransaction;

public class MainActivity extends AppCompatActivity {
    @Override
    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main);

         // Load the ExampleFragment
        if (savedInstanceState == null) {
            ExampleFragment exampleFragment = new ExampleFragment();
            FragmentTransaction transaction = getSupportFragmentManager().beginTransaction();
            transaction.replace(R.id.main_container, exampleFragment);
            transaction.commit();
        }
    }
}
```

**Note**: If your Activity already has a Toolbar set up as its ActionBar, setting the Fragment's Toolbar as the ActionBar will replace the Activity's Toolbar. If you want both toolbars to coexist, consider using a separate Toolbar without setting it as the ActionBar.

## 6. Additional Considerations

### Handling Back Navigation

If you set the Fragment's Toolbar to show the "Up" button (back arrow), ensure that you handle its behavior appropriately, typically by popping the back stack or finishing the Activity.

### Styling the Toolbar

Customize the Toolbar's appearance by defining styles or themes, adjusting colors, text sizes, and adding navigation icons as needed.

### Avoiding Toolbar Conflicts

If multiple Fragments set their own Toolbars as the ActionBar, ensure proper management of the ActionBar state to prevent conflicts. You might need to reset the Activity's original Toolbar when navigating away from the Fragment.

## Alternative Approach: Using Toolbar Without Setting It as ActionBar

If you prefer the Fragment's Toolbar to be independent of the Activity's ActionBar, you can skip setting it as the support ActionBar and manage it independently.

```java
// ExampleFragment.java (Alternative Approach)
@Override
public View onCreateView(@NonNull LayoutInflater inflater, ViewGroup container,
                         Bundle savedInstanceState) {
    View view = inflater.inflate(R.layout.fragment_example, container, false);

    toolbar = view.findViewById(R.id.toolbar_fragment);
    toolbar.setTitle("Independent Toolbar");
    toolbar.setNavigationIcon(R.drawable.ic_back);
    toolbar.setNavigationOnClickListener(v -> {
        if (getActivity() != null) {
            getActivity().onBackPressed();
        }
    });

    toolbar.inflateMenu(R.menu.fragment_menu);
    toolbar.setOnMenuItemClickListener(item -> {
        switch (item.getItemId()) {
            case R.id.action_settings:
                // Handle settings
                return true;
            case R.id.action_search:
                // Handle search
                return true;
            default:
                return false;
        }
    });

    return view;
}
```

**Pros**:
- The Fragment's Toolbar is entirely independent.
- No interference with the Activity's ActionBar.

**Cons**:
- Less integrated with the Activity's navigation and menu handling.

## Conclusion

Adding a Toolbar to a Fragment enhances your app's UI by providing contextual actions and navigation specific to the Fragment. Depending on your design requirements, you can choose to integrate the Fragment's Toolbar with the Activity's ActionBar or manage it independently. Always ensure that toolbars are managed properly to maintain a seamless user experience and avoid UI conflicts.

Certainly! Below is a JavaScript function that searches for an object by its `name` property deep within a given object. The function performs an iterative deep search (using a stack for depth-first search) without using recursion and returns the path to the found object as an array of keys.

```javascript
/**
 * Finds an object by its 'name' property deep inside a given object without using recursion.
 * Returns the path to the object as an array of keys, or null if not found.
 *
 * @param {Object} root - The root object to start the search from.
 * @param {string} targetName - The 'name' property value to search for.
 * @returns {Array<string> | null} - The path as an array of keys, or null if not found.
 */
function findObjectByName(root, targetName) {
    if (typeof root !== 'object' || root === null) {
        return null;
    }

    // Stack for DFS: each item is an object with current node and the path to it
    const stack = [{ node: root, path: [] }];

    // To handle cyclic references, keep track of visited nodes
    const visited = new Set();

    while (stack.length > 0) {
        const { node, path } = stack.pop();

        if (node && typeof node === 'object') {
            // Avoid processing the same node multiple times
            if (visited.has(node)) {
                continue;
            }
            visited.add(node);

            // Check if current node has the target name
            if (node.name === targetName) {
                return path;
            }

            // Iterate over the properties of the current node
            for (const key of Object.keys(node)) {
                const child = node[key];
                if (typeof child === 'object' && child !== null) {
                    // Push child node and updated path to the stack
                    stack.push({
                        node: child,
                        path: path.concat(key)
                    });
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // If the object with targetName is not found
    return null;
}

// Example Usage:

const data = {
    name: 'root',
    child1: {
        name: 'child1',
        grandchild: {
            name: 'grandchild1'
        }
    },
    child2: {
        name: 'child2',
        grandchild: {
            name: 'grandchild2',
            greatGrandchild: {
                name: 'targetObject'
            }
        }
    }
};

const targetName = 'targetObject';
const path = findObjectByName(data, targetName);

if (path) {
    console.log(`Path to '${targetName}':`, path.join(' -> '));
} else {
    console.log(`Object with name '${targetName}' not found.`);
}

// Output:
// Path to 'targetObject': child2 -> grandchild -> greatGrandchild
```

### Explanation:

1. **Function Definition**:
    - The function `findObjectByName` takes two parameters:
        - `root`: The object from which the search begins.
        - `targetName`: The value of the `name` property you're searching for.
    - It returns an array representing the path to the found object or `null` if not found.

2. **Stack Initialization**:
    - A stack is used to perform an iterative depth-first search (DFS).
    - Each stack element is an object containing:
        - `node`: The current object being inspected.
        - `path`: An array representing the keys traversed to reach the current node.

3. **Visited Set**:
    - To prevent infinite loops in case of cyclic references, a `Set` named `visited` keeps track of already inspected objects.

4. **Search Loop**:
    - Continues until the stack is empty.
    - Pops the last element from the stack to inspect.
    - If the current node has the `name` property matching `targetName`, the current path is returned.
    - Otherwise, it iterates through all the object's own enumerable properties.
    - If a property is an object, it's added to the stack with an updated path.

5. **Handling Not Found**:
    - If the loop completes without finding the target, the function returns `null`.

6. **Example Usage**:
    - Demonstrates how to use the function with a sample nested object.
    - Prints the path to the target object if found.

### Notes:

- **Iterative vs Recursive**: This approach avoids the potential stack overflow issues associated with deep or highly nested objects that could cause a recursive approach to fail.

- **Path Representation**: The path is represented as an array of keys that lead from the root object to the target object. In the example, the path `['child2', 'grandchild', 'greatGrandchild']` signifies accessing `data.child2.grandchild.greatGrandchild`.

- **Cyclic References**: The `visited` set is crucial for handling objects that reference themselves, directly or indirectly, preventing infinite loops during the search.

Feel free to integrate this function into your projects and modify it as needed based on your specific requirements!

