Extracting hashtags from a text string in C# can be efficiently achieved using Regular Expressions (Regex). Below is a step-by-step guide, including a sample C# program that demonstrates how to perform this task.

## What is a Hashtag?

A **hashtag** is a word or phrase preceded by the `#` symbol, used on social media platforms to categorize content. For example, in the sentence:

> "Loving the weather today! #sunny #happy"

`#sunny` and `#happy` are hashtags.

## Using Regular Expressions to Extract Hashtags

Regular Expressions (Regex) are patterns used to match character combinations in strings. In C#, the `System.Text.RegularExpressions` namespace provides classes for working with Regex.

### Regex Pattern for Hashtags

A simple regex pattern to match hashtags can be:

```regex
#\w+
```

- `#`: Matches the literal `#` symbol.
- `\w+`: Matches one or more word characters (equivalent to `[A-Za-z0-9_]`).

**Note:** Depending on your requirements, you might need a more sophisticated pattern to handle hashtags with special characters or Unicode.

## Sample C# Code

Here's a complete example demonstrating how to extract hashtags from a given text:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
using System.Collections.Generic;

class HashtagExtractor
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string inputText = "Loving the weather today! #sunny #happy #CSharp #100DaysOfCode";

        // Define the regex pattern for hashtags
        string pattern = @"#\w+";

        // Create a Regex object
        Regex regex = new Regex(pattern);

        // Find all matches in the input text
        MatchCollection matches = regex.Matches(inputText);

        // Create a list to store hashtags
        List<string> hashtags = new List<string>();

        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            // Optionally, remove the '#' if you only want the word
            string hashtag = match.Value;
            // Remove '#' if not needed
            // hashtag = hashtag.Substring(1);

            hashtags.Add(hashtag);
        }

        // Display the extracted hashtags
        Console.WriteLine("Extracted Hashtags:");
        foreach (string tag in hashtags)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(tag);
        }
    }
}
```

### Output

```
Extracted Hashtags:
#sunny
#happy
#CSharp
#100DaysOfCode
```

## Explanation

1. **Input Text:** Define the string from which you want to extract hashtags.

2. **Regex Pattern:**
   - `#\w+`: Matches any sequence that starts with `#` followed by one or more word characters.

3. **Regex Object:**
   - `Regex regex = new Regex(pattern);`: Creates a Regex object with the defined pattern.

4. **Finding Matches:**
   - `MatchCollection matches = regex.Matches(inputText);`: Finds all matches of the pattern in the input text.

5. **Storing Hashtags:**
   - Iterates through each match and adds it to a list. You can choose to remove the `#` if only the tag is needed by uncommenting the `Substring` line.

6. **Displaying Results:**
   - Prints out all extracted hashtags.

## Handling More Complex Hashtags

If your hashtags may include Unicode characters or other special characters (like hyphens), you might need a more advanced regex pattern. Here's an example:

```csharp
string pattern = @"#\p{L}[\p{L}\p{N}_-]*";
```

- `\p{L}`: Matches any Unicode letter.
- `[\p{L}\p{N}_-]*`: Matches zero or more Unicode letters, numbers, underscores, or hyphens.

This pattern allows hashtags like `#C#` or `#Happy-Day`.

## Using LINQ for Extraction (Alternative Method)

You can also use LINQ combined with Regex for a more concise approach:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
using System.Linq;

class HashtagExtractorLINQ
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string inputText = "Loving the weather today! #sunny #happy #CSharp #100DaysOfCode";

        var hashtags = Regex.Matches(inputText, @"#\w+")
                            .Cast<Match>()
                            .Select(m => m.Value)
                            .ToList();

        Console.WriteLine("Extracted Hashtags:");
        foreach (var tag in hashtags)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(tag);
        }
    }
}
```

This approach provides the same output with more concise code using LINQ.

## Conclusion

Extracting hashtags from text in C# is straightforward with the use of Regular Expressions. Depending on the complexity of the hashtags you expect in your input, you can adjust the regex pattern accordingly. The examples provided should serve as a solid foundation for implementing hashtag extraction in your C# applications.

To add the "Add User" functionality to your button, you'll need to use JavaScript to handle the button click event, retrieve the user input, and make a POST request to your API. Below is a step-by-step guide along with the necessary code snippets to achieve this.

### 1. Update Your HTML

First, add an `id` to your **Add User** button to easily reference it in JavaScript. Additionally, you can include some elements to display success or error messages to the user.

```html
<div class="container-fluid px-md-5">
    <div class="row">
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h5 class="card-title">Add last used RFID card as new user</h5>
                <p class="card-text">
                    Card: <strong>{{ latest_key[:8] + "..." + latest_key[-8:]}}</strong> was triggered at: <strong>20:57AM</strong>
                </p>
                <div class="input-group mb-3">
                    <input type="text" class="form-control" id="user_name" placeholder="User Name">
                    <button class="btn btn-primary" type="button" id="addUserButton">
                        <i class="bi bi-person-add"></i> Add User
                    </button>
                </div>
                <!-- Alert Messages -->
                <div id="alertPlaceholder"></div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="py-3">
        <table id="userTable" class="table table-striped table-bordered" style="width:100%">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>User</th>
                    <th>User Key</th>
                    <th>Permissions</th>
                    <th>Operation</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <!-- Existing Users Will Be Listed Here -->
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</div>
```

**Changes Made:**

1. **Added `id="addUserButton"`** to the button for easy JavaScript referencing.
2. **Placed the input and button in the desired order** (input first, then button) to follow conventional UX patterns.
3. **Added a `div` with `id="alertPlaceholder"`** to display success or error messages.

### 2. Add JavaScript to Handle the Button Click

Place the following JavaScript code at the end of your HTML file, just before the closing `</body>` tag, or in a separate JavaScript file linked to your HTML.

```html
<script>
// Wait until the DOM is fully loaded
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    const addUserButton = document.getElementById('addUserButton');
    const userNameInput = document.getElementById('user_name');
    const alertPlaceholder = document.getElementById('alertPlaceholder');
    const userTableBody = document.querySelector('#userTable tbody');

    // Fixed user key
    const USER_KEY = 'd2db5ec4-6e7a-11ee-b962-0242ac120002';

    // Function to display alerts
    function showAlert(message, type) {
        const wrapper = document.createElement('div');
        wrapper.innerHTML = [
            `<div class="alert alert-${type} alert-dismissible" role="alert">`,
            `   ${message}`,
            '   <button type="button" class="btn-close" data-bs-dismiss="alert" aria-label="Close"></button>',
            '</div>'
        ].join('');
        alertPlaceholder.innerHTML = ''; // Clear previous alerts
        alertPlaceholder.append(wrapper);
    }

    // Function to add a new row to the table
    function addUserToTable(userName, userKey) {
        const row = document.createElement('tr');

        // Create table cells
        const userCell = document.createElement('td');
        userCell.textContent = userName;

        const keyCell = document.createElement('td');
        keyCell.textContent = userKey;

        const permissionsCell = document.createElement('td');
        permissionsCell.textContent = 'Default Permissions'; // Customize as needed

        const operationCell = document.createElement('td');
        // Add operation buttons or links here if needed
        operationCell.innerHTML = '<button class="btn btn-sm btn-danger">Delete</button>';

        // Append cells to the row
        row.appendChild(userCell);
        row.appendChild(keyCell);
        row.appendChild(permissionsCell);
        row.appendChild(operationCell);

        // Append the row to the table body
        userTableBody.appendChild(row);
    }

    // Event listener for the Add User button
    addUserButton.addEventListener('click', () => {
        const userName = userNameInput.value.trim();

        // Validate input
        if (!userName) {
            showAlert('Please enter a user name.', 'warning');
            return;
        }

        // Construct the API URL
        const apiUrl = `http://localhost:5000/api/users/${encodeURIComponent(userName)}/devices/${USER_KEY}`;

        // Disable the button to prevent multiple clicks
        addUserButton.disabled = true;

        // Make the POST request
        fetch(apiUrl, {
            method: 'POST'
        })
        .then(response => {
            if (!response.ok) {
                throw new Error(`Error: ${response.status} ${response.statusText}`);
            }
            return response.json(); // Assuming the API returns JSON
        })
        .then(data => {
            // Handle successful response
            showAlert(`User "${userName}" has been added successfully.`, 'success');

            // Optionally, add the new user to the table
            addUserToTable(userName, USER_KEY);

            // Clear the input field
            userNameInput.value = '';
        })
        .catch(error => {
            // Handle errors
            showAlert(`Failed to add user: ${error.message}`, 'danger');
            console.error('Error adding user:', error);
        })
        .finally(() => {
            // Re-enable the button
            addUserButton.disabled = false;
        });
    });
});
</script>
```

**Explanation of the JavaScript Code:**

1. **Event Listener Setup:**
   - The script waits for the DOM to load before executing to ensure all elements are available.

2. **Element References:**
   - `addUserButton`: The **Add User** button.
   - `userNameInput`: The input field where the user enters the name.
   - `alertPlaceholder`: The div where alert messages will be displayed.
   - `userTableBody`: The `<tbody>` of your users table where new users will be appended.

3. **Fixed User Key:**
   - `USER_KEY` is set to the constant value provided.

4. **`showAlert` Function:**
   - Creates and displays Bootstrap-styled alert messages for success, warning, and error notifications.

5. **`addUserToTable` Function:**
   - Dynamically adds a new row to the users table with the provided user name and user key.
   - You can customize the **Permissions** and **Operation** columns as needed.

6. **Button Click Event Handler:**
   - **Validation:** Checks if the user name input is not empty.
   - **API Call:** Constructs the API endpoint using the entered user name and the fixed user key, then makes a POST request using the `fetch` API.
   - **Handling Responses:**
     - On success: Shows a success alert, adds the new user to the table, and clears the input field.
     - On failure: Shows an error alert with the error message.
   - **Button State:** Disables the button while the request is in progress to prevent duplicate submissions and re-enables it afterward.

### 3. (Optional) Initialize Existing Users in the Table

If you have existing users that you'd like to display when the page loads, you'll need to populate the table accordingly. This typically involves fetching user data from your backend and iterating over it to create table rows. Here's a basic example:

```html
<script>
// ... (Previous JavaScript code)

document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    // Function to fetch and display existing users
    function loadExistingUsers() {
        fetch('http://localhost:5000/api/users') // Adjust the endpoint as needed
            .then(response => {
                if (!response.ok) {
                    throw new Error(`Error: ${response.status} ${response.statusText}`);
                }
                return response.json();
            })
            .then(users => {
                users.forEach(user => {
                    addUserToTable(user.name, user.key);
                });
            })
            .catch(error => {
                showAlert(`Failed to load users: ${error.message}`, 'danger');
                console.error('Error loading users:', error);
            });
    }

    // Call the function to load users
    loadExistingUsers();
});
</script>
```

**Note:** Ensure that your backend API has an endpoint like `GET /api/users` that returns a list of existing users in JSON format. Adjust the endpoint and the data handling as per your API's structure.

### 4. Ensure CORS is Configured (If Needed)

If your frontend is served from a different origin than your backend API (e.g., different ports), ensure that your backend is configured to handle Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS). In a Flask backend, you can use the `flask-cors` extension:

```python
from flask import Flask
from flask_cors import CORS

app = Flask(__name__)
CORS(app)  # This will enable CORS for all routes
```

Install it using pip if you haven't already:

```bash
pip install flask-cors
```

### 5. Test the Implementation

1. **Start Your Backend Server:** Ensure that your API is running and accessible at `http://localhost:5000`.
2. **Open Your HTML Page:** Load the HTML page in your browser.
3. **Add a New User:**
   - Enter a user name in the input field.
   - Click the **Add User** button.
   - Observe the success or error message.
   - Verify that the new user appears in the table if the operation was successful.

### 6. Additional Enhancements

- **Form Submission on Enter Key:** Allow form submission when the user presses the Enter key.
- **Input Validation:** Implement more robust validation (e.g., allowed characters, length checks).
- **Loading Indicators:** Show a spinner or loading indicator while the API request is in progress.
- **Error Handling Enhancements:** Display specific error messages based on the API's response.

**Example: Submitting the form with the Enter key**

You can wrap the input and button inside a `<form>` and listen for the form's `submit` event:

```html
<form id="addUserForm">
    <div class="input-group mb-3">
        <input type="text" class="form-control" id="user_name" placeholder="User Name" required>
        <button class="btn btn-primary" type="submit" id="addUserButton">
            <i class="bi bi-person-add"></i> Add User
        </button>
    </div>
</form>
```

Update the JavaScript to handle the form submission:

```javascript
const addUserForm = document.getElementById('addUserForm');

addUserForm.addEventListener('submit', (event) => {
    event.preventDefault(); // Prevent the default form submission
    addUserButton.click();  // Trigger the button click event
});
```

This allows users to add a new user by pressing the Enter key after typing the user name.

